  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 PM EDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nChattanooga's streak of 90F+ continues. Unfortunately my advanced \ntechnique of shaking fist at Sun is not working to relieve our \ncollective burden of summer overstaying its welcome. Really there's \nno significant changes in the forecast cycle. Can't rule out a \nrandom cumulus cloud letting out a shower over the Plateau/tall \nwestern ridges today, then we turn to a diurnally forced scattered \nshower/thunderstorm pattern as the ridge gradually begins its \nwestern trek and better atmospheric moisture returns to our local \nvertical profiles. Friday into next week are best chances for \nscattered showers and storms, again with it being this hot out, \nthink summer pattern where valley locations are both unpredictable \nand may require some luck to get rainfall.\n\nThe bummer aspect of diurnally forced storms is the best coverage is \nusually right at or right after peak heating, so not particularly \nexpecting storms to prevent highs from occurring, and more likely\nto occur in the high Plateau or mountain terrain. Also any relief\nfrom temperatures from afternoon storms comes with the tradeoff of\npost storm humidity. \n\nNext week with the upper ridge centered to our west but still \ngenerally above average H500 heights, we'll be above normal and hot \n(I am so sorry Chattanooga but you're still stuck in the 90s) but\nslightly less hot than right now. I don't think we'll see the \nsame and definitely not identical coverage of storms each day over\nthe next week, but everyone will have at least an opportunity to\nwet their driveways. As far as weather hazards out of the storms,\nboth bulk shear and midlevel lapse rates don't appear to be of \nsignificance over the next week, so really the only hazards would\nbe typical thunderstorms: lightning, maybe a gusty wind, and a \nnonzero risk for heavy rains.",
